.TH SH-FEM 1
.SH NAME
fem \- shell script interface to fem(2)'s problem-specification language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load fem

.B ${fem
.B new
.B }
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B mesh
.I NXxNYxNZ
.RB [ domain
.IR WxHxD ]
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B material diffusivity
.I D
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B load
.I Q
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B bc dirichlet
.I V
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B solver gmres|cg
.RB [ tolerance
.IR T ]
.RB [ restart
.IR R ]
.RB [ maxiter
.IR N ]
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B backend cpu|gpu
.RB [ precision
.IR f32|f64 ]
.RB [ resident
.IR on|off ]
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B commit
.br
.B fem
.I id
.B solve
.br
.B ${fem
.I id
.B get
.I n
.B }
.br
.B ${fem
.I id
.B getijk
.I i j k
.B }
.br
.B ${fem
.I id
.B nnodes|iters|resid|lasterror
.B }
.br
.B fem del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fem
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR fem (2)'s
own little language for specifying a whole finite-element Poisson
problem \(em mesh, material, load, boundary condition, solver, and
execution backend together \(em declaratively, instead of a compiled
Limbo program.
.PP
.IR Fem (2)'s
own
.B newproblem
takes a whole spec as one string in a single call; this module adds
the incremental, script-friendly build-up around it \(em
.BR mesh / material / load / bc / solver / backend
accumulate one line of the spec each,
.B commit
then calls
.IR fem (2)'s
.B newproblem
on the accumulated lines, and
.B solve
calls
.IR fem (2)'s
own
.B solve
\(em without changing
.IR fem (2)
itself in any way: every verb below except the handle/solution
bookkeeping is exactly
.IR fem (2)'s
(and, for
.BR backend ", " gpu (2)'s)
own vocabulary, documented there in full.
.PP
.B ${fem new}
allocates a handle for a problem still being built, and yields it.
.BR Mesh ,
.BR material ,
.BR load ,
.BR bc ,
.BR solver ,
and
.B backend
each append one line of spec text to that handle; using any of them
after
.B commit
raises an error.
.B Commit
parses the accumulated spec via
.IR fem (2)'s
.B newproblem
(real assembly happens here, not at
.BR solve );
.B solve
then runs
.IR fem (2)'s
own
.B solve
and stores the result on the handle. Using
.BR get ,
.BR getijk ,
.BR nnodes ,
.BR iters ,
.BR resid ,
or
.B lasterror
before committing (or, for the solution-dependent verbs, before
solving) raises an error.
.PP
.BR Get
reads the solved value at flat node
.IR n ;
.B getijk
is the same by grid position, via
.IR femesh (2)'s
own
.BR nodeid .
.B Nnodes
is the mesh's node count.
.B Iters
and
.B resid
are the solve's own iteration count and final residual.
.B Lasterror
reads
.IR p . backend . lasterror
\(em nil (the empty string) unless a requested
.B backend
line fell back to a different one; see
.IR gpu (2).
.B Del
frees one or more handles.
.PP
A complete example \(em an implicit Poisson solve on a small cube,
requesting (and, in this build, gracefully falling back from) a gpu
backend:
.EX
	load fem
	id := ${fem new}
	fem $id mesh 20x20x20 domain 1x1x1
	fem $id material diffusivity 1.0
	fem $id load 1.0
	fem $id bc dirichlet 0.0
	fem $id solver cg tolerance 1e-8
	fem $id backend gpu precision f32
	fem $id commit
	fem $id solve
	echo iters ${fem $id iters} lasterror ${fem $id lasterror}
	echo centre ${fem $id getijk 10 10 10}
	fem del $id
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/fem.b
